    "scan_summary": "The structural analysis of the Lantronix EDS5000 firmware (v2.3.0.0R7) reveals a high-risk attack surface characterized by several custom vendor binaries (diresponder, ltrx_cli, dsrcts_server) running as root with active network listeners and file-write capabilities. Most notably, the 'nuevo80' service utilizes a CLI binary containing strings that suggest the use of unsanitized shell commands (e.g., openssl, grep /etc/shadow), indicating a high probability of both arbitrary file write and command injection. Standard OpenWrt components (netifd, odhcpd) are also present and privileged, though they represent a lower risk relative to the custom vendor-developed services.",
    "findings": [
      {
        "finding_id": "F001",
        "title": "Unauthenticated Privileged File Write in Discovery Service",
        "explanation": "The discovery responder binary 'diresponder' runs as the root user and listens for network traffic (bind/recvmsg). It lacks symbols for authentication and privilege dropping, while importing functions that write to the filesystem. The presence of strings related to PID files and IPC sockets in /tmp suggests it manages state files that could be targeted for symlink attacks or arbitrary content injection if network-provided data is not sanitized.",
        "priority": "High",
        "attack_scenario": "An attacker sends a crafted UDP discovery packet to the service's listening port to trigger the creation or overwrite of files in /tmp or /var/run, potentially leading to a Denial of Service or hijacking of IPC mechanisms.",
        "permanent_fix": "Modify the diresponder service to run as a non-privileged user and implement strict validation/sanitization of all network-received data before using it in file operations.",
        "immediate_mitigation": "Disable the discovery service if not required for deployment, or use a firewall to restrict access to the discovery ports to trusted management subnets only.",
        "compromise_indicators": "Unexpected file ownership or modified timestamps for files in /tmp/ipc/ or /tmp/diresponder.info, or the presence of non-standard files in /var/run/."
      },
      {
        "finding_id": "F002",
        "title": "Privileged Network Configuration Service Risk",
        "explanation": "The netifd daemon is a core OpenWrt component running as root that manages network interfaces and writes configuration files like resolv.conf. While it is a standard component, its privileged status and interaction with network-derived data (e.g., via DHCP) mean that any vulnerability in its path handling or file renaming logic could allow for unauthorized system configuration changes.",
        "priority": "Medium",
        "attack_scenario": "An attacker on the local network provides a malicious DHCP response containing crafted hostnames or options designed to cause netifd to perform directory traversal when writing its temporary resolution files.",
        "permanent_fix": "Ensure the service uses a restricted filesystem namespace or AppArmor/Selinux profile to limit file writes to a specific set of configuration directories.",
        "immediate_mitigation": "Enable DHCP snooping on the local network switch to prevent unauthorized DHCP servers from providing malicious configuration data to the device.",
        "compromise_indicators": "Modification of /etc/board.json or unexpected entries in /tmp/resolv.conf.d/ that do not match the expected network environment."
